[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the OSPF process on CSW1, specifically the interface policy for eth0 facing R1. Mechanistically, OSPF adjacencies do not form on interfaces configured as passive because Hello packets are suppressed. That exactly matches what we see: both sides can ping (L3 up), but no OSPF neighbor appears.

Localization evidence:
- On CSW1, “show ip ospf interface json” for eth0 (10.0.0.34/30 toward R1) shows timerPassiveIface: true, nbrCount: 0, and state: DR (it elects itself DR because it never sees hellos). Running config explicitly has under “interface eth0”: ip ospf passive.
- On R1, “show ip ospf interface json” for eth0 (10.0.0.33/30 toward CSW1) shows OSPF enabled with nbrCount: 0 and state: DR, with no passive flag set. So R1 is sending hellos but receives none from CSW1.

Root cause: A recent configuration change on CSW1 inadvertently set eth0 as an OSPF passive interface, preventing OSPF hellos to R1.

Corrective fix:
- On CSW1, remove the passive setting on the R1-facing link:
  - Interface method: interface eth0; no ip ospf passive
  - Or in router ospf: no passive-interface eth0 (if passive-interface default is used)
After this change, verify that “nbrCount” increments and adjacency forms (Full) on both CSW1 eth0 and R1 eth0, restoring dual-path OSPF to R1. === END ===